WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.050 --> 00:00:05.630
The holiday classic How The Grinch Stole Christmas
by Dr Seuss has been given an animated makeover

00:00:05.630 --> 00:00:08.060
by the team behind the Despicable Me movies.

00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:12.440
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, it's Jan here
and today I'm explaining the biggest differences

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:17.810
between Illumination's new Grinch movie, the
60s animated TV special, and the live-action

00:00:17.810 --> 00:00:19.119
film with Jim Carrey!

00:00:19.119 --> 00:00:22.070
Keep watching to the end for a bonus Grinch
easter egg!

00:00:22.070 --> 00:00:26.039
And subscribe and leave a comment about the
movie for a chance to win some awesome Grinch

00:00:26.039 --> 00:00:27.269
merchandise.

00:00:27.269 --> 00:00:30.979
The first big difference that you'll notice
between this particular Grinch and previous

00:00:30.979 --> 00:00:36.070
versions is that the filmmakers have deliberately
made him a little more likeable and relatable.

00:00:36.070 --> 00:00:40.250
That's because they felt that a very nasty
Grinch would be too off-putting in a feature-length

00:00:40.250 --> 00:00:45.010
festive film, so they sanded down his spiky
edges somewhat.

00:00:45.010 --> 00:00:49.219
Another big change in this version is that
the filmmakers delve into why the Grinch became

00:00:49.219 --> 00:00:50.609
the way he is.

00:00:50.609 --> 00:00:55.180
In the original book and made-for-TV animation,
the Grinch didn't have any real backstory.

00:00:55.180 --> 00:01:00.370
We just knew he'd hated Christmas for 53 years,
likely because his "heart was two sizes too

00:01:00.370 --> 00:01:01.370
small."

00:01:01.370 --> 00:01:05.770
The live-action film, however, did add some
background information with baby Grinch arriving

00:01:05.770 --> 00:01:10.560
by accident in Whoville, getting adopted by
elderly women, falling for a girl at school,

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:14.680
and being bullied by his classmates, which
made the young Grinch leave town for Mount

00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:15.830
Crumpit.

00:01:15.830 --> 00:01:20.320
Like that movie, the new animation returns
to the Grinch's youth for answers, though

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:24.390
this time, his emotional pain stems from a
childhood spent in an orphanage where he was

00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:26.710
basically abandoned during Christmas.

00:01:26.710 --> 00:01:31.740
By bringing up the Grinch's backstory, the
filmmakers wanted to soften our feelings towards

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:35.640
him and open up a path for him to heal over
the course of the story.

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:39.780
Indeed, according to Benedict Cumberbatch,
'once you understand why Christmas is painful

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:42.170
for him, you kind of root for him a little.'

00:01:42.170 --> 00:01:44.430
But what about the new Grinch's appearance?

00:01:44.430 --> 00:01:49.600
Well, although, in his 1957 book, he started
out black and white with pinky-coloured eyes,

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:55.560
thanks to the 1966 animation, green is very
much the colour associated with the holiday-hating

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:56.560
character.

00:01:56.560 --> 00:02:01.040
And, as you'd expect, just like the live-action
movie in 2000, Illumination's animated feature

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:05.870
sticks with green, though it does make some
changes, giving his teeth a cosmetic makeover

00:02:05.870 --> 00:02:11.090
and adding pinker lips and green on white
eyes, in a change from the pinky-red on yellow

00:02:11.090 --> 00:02:15.420
eyes of the 60s animation and the green on
yellow eyes of the live-action.

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:20.810
This Grinch also looks brighter, softer, better
groomed and less creepy than previous versions,

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:23.980
which I imagine was done to make him look
more generally appealing.

00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:28.390
The amazing progress in animation tools and
techniques since the early cartoon means that

00:02:28.390 --> 00:02:32.540
there's a real sense of texture to the Grinch's
hair in this new film, and the wavy effect

00:02:32.540 --> 00:02:36.700
in his fur feels like a nod to the character's
drawings in the book.

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:40.870
As far as the other characters go, the filmmakers
have gone for a less zany look that's more

00:02:40.870 --> 00:02:46.010
human and largely cuter than in previous adaptations
as they wanted the movie to appeal to a broad

00:02:46.010 --> 00:02:51.090
audience including people who'd never seen
the original Dr Seuss and Chuck Jones artwork.

00:02:51.090 --> 00:02:54.490
The Grinch's dog Max has been part of his
story since the original book.

00:02:54.490 --> 00:02:59.240
But in this new version, the relationship
between Max and the Grinch is much more developed.

00:02:59.240 --> 00:03:03.790
Max isn't just a pet or substitute reindeer;
he's everything to the Grinch including his

00:03:03.790 --> 00:03:08.510
best friend, his pretty much constant companion,
and his devoted right-hand dog.

00:03:08.510 --> 00:03:13.340
In fact, Max's incredible loyalty to his green
friend is designed to help us to see the goodness

00:03:13.340 --> 00:03:15.060
in the Grinch.

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:18.790
Someone else who's been part of the grouchy
green character's story from the very beginning

00:03:18.790 --> 00:03:20.450
is Cindy-Lou Who.

00:03:20.450 --> 00:03:25.640
In the book and TV special, she's just 2 years
old and her only real interaction with the

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:29.730
Grinch is when she chances upon him while
he's disguised as Santa and asks why he's

00:03:29.730 --> 00:03:32.120
taking her family's Christmas tree.

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:37.240
The new movie, like the Jim Carrey film, ages
Cindy-Lou up several years and greatly expands

00:03:37.240 --> 00:03:38.420
her storyline.

00:03:38.420 --> 00:03:43.599
In the live-action, Cindy-Lou has more interaction
with the Grinch than this film, though Illumination's

00:03:43.599 --> 00:03:48.020
movie still gives her an active role which
involves her trying to trap Santa as she wants

00:03:48.020 --> 00:03:51.240
him to help out her tired, over-worked mother.

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:54.810
There's also a whole host of new characters
introduced in this film.

00:03:54.810 --> 00:03:59.480
Not content with just having one adorable
creature on screen, the new movie adds a reindeer

00:03:59.480 --> 00:04:03.870
called Fred who I can absolutely see being
a favourite with kids and selling a ton of

00:04:03.870 --> 00:04:05.910
toys, much like Max.

00:04:05.910 --> 00:04:10.150
Fred appears when the Grinch starts to put
together his plot to steal Christmas and he's

00:04:10.150 --> 00:04:15.300
not only a nice comic foil to the Grinch's
grumpiness but, as with Max, he also lets

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:18.849
us see a different side to the green Christmas
thief.

00:04:18.849 --> 00:04:23.069
Another new animal addition to the story is
a wild goat, and although he's not on screen

00:04:23.069 --> 00:04:27.860
very long, he definitely leaves an impression
thanks to his rather distinctive voice, which

00:04:27.860 --> 00:04:30.430
adds to the film's comedy!

00:04:30.430 --> 00:04:35.470
Another new face in Whoville is the Grinch's
nearest neighbour, Mr Bricklebaum.

00:04:35.470 --> 00:04:39.610
He's the "happiest Who alive" and considers
himself to be the Grinch's best friend, though

00:04:39.610 --> 00:04:42.800
the Grinch, of course, has an entirely different
view of the situation!

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:47.379
Bricklebaum, who's voiced by Saturday Night
Live star Kenan Thompson, is full of Christmas

00:04:47.379 --> 00:04:52.009
spirit and just adores covering his house
in lights and breaking out his huge selection

00:04:52.009 --> 00:04:55.490
of Santa and Snowman inflatables to celebrate
the holidays.

00:04:55.490 --> 00:05:00.259
Basically, he's another obstacle in the way
of the Grinch's isolation and the Grinch doesn't

00:05:00.259 --> 00:05:02.379
like it one bit!

00:05:02.379 --> 00:05:05.729
Also not in the original book or TV special
is the Mayor of Whoville.

00:05:05.729 --> 00:05:09.719
Now, if you know the live-action version,
you'll remember there was a mayor in that

00:05:09.719 --> 00:05:12.129
film and he was an entirely nasty piece of
work.

00:05:12.129 --> 00:05:16.069
In fact, he was the reason why the Grinch
cut himself off from society.

00:05:16.069 --> 00:05:20.789
However, in Illumination's film, the mayor
isn't a baddie, she's a completely different

00:05:20.789 --> 00:05:24.990
character, and it's a real treat to hear her
played by Angela Lansbury.

00:05:24.990 --> 00:05:28.979
While Cindy-Lou's parents aren't mentioned
in the book or TV animation, they do make

00:05:28.979 --> 00:05:33.289
an appearance in the live-action movie, where
her father is the town's postmaster and her

00:05:33.289 --> 00:05:35.870
mother competes to have the best Christmas
lights in Whoville.

00:05:35.870 --> 00:05:40.559
In a complete change, in the new animation,
Cindy-Lou's mother, Donna, is a frazzled,

00:05:40.559 --> 00:05:45.229
hard-working single parent with three children
and she's voiced by Parks &amp; Rec star Rashida

00:05:45.229 --> 00:05:46.460
Jones.

00:05:46.460 --> 00:05:50.469
Similar to the live-action father, though,
this new animated mother realises that Christmas

00:05:50.469 --> 00:05:53.339
is about much more than things.

00:05:53.339 --> 00:05:57.810
Cindy-Lou needs some help to carry out her
plan to trap Santa, so she turns to her friends,

00:05:57.810 --> 00:06:00.339
who are all new additions to the popular story.

00:06:00.339 --> 00:06:04.559
There's her best friend Groopert as well as
the brains of the outfit, Izzy, the laid-back

00:06:04.559 --> 00:06:07.509
Axl, and the wisecracking Ozzy.

00:06:07.509 --> 00:06:11.430
In the book, there's only a handful of houses
shown in Whoville, and although the number

00:06:11.430 --> 00:06:16.770
of colourful Who homes more or less triples
in the TV special, it's still a tiny town.

00:06:16.770 --> 00:06:20.889
In the live-action movie, Whoville got much
bigger and more detailed with a post office,

00:06:20.889 --> 00:06:25.779
stores, and a school, and there was also an
excess of festive lights, ornaments, and presents

00:06:25.779 --> 00:06:28.759
as the Whos were more materialistic.

00:06:28.759 --> 00:06:33.080
Illumination's new film takes things in a
different direction, making Whoville's houses,

00:06:33.080 --> 00:06:37.710
stores, businesses, and vehicles reflect the
warm and welcoming nature of the Whos.

00:06:37.710 --> 00:06:41.339
The new Whoville is a vibrant town full of
vivid colours.

00:06:41.339 --> 00:06:45.849
In fact, it appears so sweet and inviting
that it looks like it could be made out of

00:06:45.849 --> 00:06:46.849
gingerbread!

00:06:46.849 --> 00:06:50.439
By the way, when creating Whoville, the filmmakers
made sure they avoided straight lines where

00:06:50.439 --> 00:06:54.000
possible to keep a similar vibe to Dr Seuss's
work.

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:58.900
Story-wise, there are some interesting differences
between all the versions of the Grinch's tale.

00:06:58.900 --> 00:07:03.180
The only time the Grinch visits Whoville in
both the book and the 60s animation is to

00:07:03.180 --> 00:07:05.619
carry out his plan to steal Christmas.

00:07:05.619 --> 00:07:09.689
In the live-action film, however, the Grinch
visited Whoville several times before trying

00:07:09.689 --> 00:07:10.869
to steal their big day.

00:07:10.869 --> 00:07:15.759
First, he went to cause trouble after some
Whos disturbed him on Mount Crumpit; and later,

00:07:15.759 --> 00:07:19.699
he returned as holiday Cheer-Meister for the
Who's seasonal celebration.

00:07:19.699 --> 00:07:24.240
The new animated movie also shows the Grinch
descending from his mountain home before his

00:07:24.240 --> 00:07:28.580
big robbery, but this time it's because he's
run out of groceries after stress-eating his

00:07:28.580 --> 00:07:31.669
way through all the food in his house ahead
of Christmas.

00:07:31.669 --> 00:07:35.949
He later ends up in Whoville again after his
plan to launch a huge snowball at the town

00:07:35.949 --> 00:07:37.360
goes wrong.

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:42.149
While all the versions of the story show how
much the Grinch hates all the noise of Christmas,

00:07:42.149 --> 00:07:47.150
the new animated movie ups the stakes as the
Grinch is faced with the prospect of the noisiest

00:07:47.150 --> 00:07:52.419
holiday season ever as, this year, Whoville's
mayor wants the festivities to be three times

00:07:52.419 --> 00:07:53.559
bigger than usual.

00:07:53.559 --> 00:07:58.710
Of course, the Grinch really can't stand the
idea of that, which leads him to steal Christmas

00:07:58.710 --> 00:08:01.089
to stop the noisy madness of it all!

00:08:01.089 --> 00:08:05.429
I mentioned earlier that Whoville's much bigger
in this film, and that means that the Grinch's

00:08:05.429 --> 00:08:09.719
plan to rob the Whos turns out to be much
more challenging than previous adaptations.

00:08:09.719 --> 00:08:14.889
In fact, this time, it's a whole big production,
more akin to a huge heist!

00:08:14.889 --> 00:08:21.360
So, expect to see the Grinch get really creative,
using a massive range of crazy Rube Goldberg-style

00:08:21.360 --> 00:08:25.160
contraptions that reminded me a lot of the
kind of stuff you see in Aardman's Wallace

00:08:25.160 --> 00:08:26.300
and Gromit films.

00:08:26.300 --> 00:08:31.419
Oh, and the Grinch's cave is also full of
ridiculous, hi-tech gadgets and gizmos too.

00:08:31.419 --> 00:08:35.690
If you're a fan of the songs from the original
animation, you'll be pleased to hear the new

00:08:35.690 --> 00:08:39.980
movie features both 'Welcome Christmas', which
gets a pretty familiar interpretation, and

00:08:39.980 --> 00:08:43.530
'You're A Mean One, Mr Grinch', which is given
a totally different spin.

00:08:43.530 --> 00:08:49.090
There's also a new song, 'I Am The Grinch',
which plays over the closing credits.

00:08:49.090 --> 00:08:52.930
Singer-songwriter Pharrell Williams, who provides
a lovely and suitably warm narration for the

00:08:52.930 --> 00:08:57.810
new film, certainly had big shoes to fill
as generations have grown up hearing Boris

00:08:57.810 --> 00:09:00.610
Karloff lend his inimitable voice to the TV
special.

00:09:00.610 --> 00:09:06.190
As for the new script, it mixes Dr Seuss's
original language with new dialogue and narration,

00:09:06.190 --> 00:09:10.910
so in addition to many classic lines, you'll
also hear new material that aims to reflect

00:09:10.910 --> 00:09:13.589
the style and spirit of the original.

00:09:13.589 --> 00:09:17.470
Pharrell's vocal performance in the Grinch
is also an Illumination easter egg as the

00:09:17.470 --> 00:09:22.030
singer-songwriter has composed themes and
songs, including the Oscar-nominated Happy,

00:09:22.030 --> 00:09:23.860
for the Despicable Me movies.

00:09:23.860 --> 00:09:28.020
And I'll be talking about lots more Illumination
easter eggs in my Things You Missed in The

00:09:28.020 --> 00:09:32.370
Grinch video which you can tap here to watch
or click the link at the end or in the video

00:09:32.370 --> 00:09:33.370
description.

00:09:33.370 --> 00:09:35.860
Now, which is your favourite version of the
Grinch?

00:09:35.860 --> 00:09:38.250
And what do you like best about the new movie?

